00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.200
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi, I'm Mike
Coleman, a developer advocate

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.250
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.050
And in this video, I
want to talk to you

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.960
about how you manage your
applications after you've

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.566
migrated them using
Migrate for Anthos.

00:00:11.566 --> 00:00:14.696
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.840
Getting your application
containerized

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.340
is just the first step.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.980
You'll also need to
consider how you manage

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.630
that application going forward.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.940
How do you handle
base operating system

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.740
updates, application patching,
and other Day Two tasks?

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.610
The rest of this video aims to
answer some of those questions.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.620
As a quick side
note, I'm not going

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.260
to spend a lot of time talking
about how container images are

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.100
constructed.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.170
If you're not familiar with
that topic, pause the video,

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.590
go check out the documentation
on the Docker site

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.390
and come back and rejoin me.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.060
Also, if you're not familiar
with Migrate for Anthos,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.650
check out our
earlier videos where

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.750
we provide an overview
of the service

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.570
as well as how to perform
an actual migration.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:04.620
You can find a link to those
in the description below.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.630
With that being said,
let's take a look

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.210
at how Migrate for Anthos
creates the container image

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.200
from a migrated application.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.440
Migrate for Anthos automatically
examines a source VM

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.555
and extracts the
workloads running there

00:01:17.555 --> 00:01:19.790
into their own container images.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.520
When looking at the ongoing
management of those containers,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.190
it's important to understand
how their images are actually

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.000
built.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.980
The container that
you run in production

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.530
is a combination of two
intermediate images.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.460
The first one is the Migrate
for Anthos runtime layer

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.030
and the other is what we refer
to as the non-runnable system

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:38.870
layer.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:42.320
The non-runnable system layer
contains the extracted portion

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.390
of your migrated
workloads and will

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.540
serve as the base for
all future updates.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:51.080
As the name implies, this layer
by itself doesn't do anything.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.810
It's only by combining it
with the Migrate for Anthos

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.660
runtime layer that you get
a container image that you

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.400
can deploy into production.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.350
So let's look at the
Dockerfile so you can

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:03.080
see how this works in practice.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.570
Now, the Dockerfile
uses a multi-stage

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.900
build to combine the
two artifacts together.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.290
This first FROM statement
tells Docker to use the Migrate

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:16.610
for Anthos 1.6.0 runtime image
from the Google Container

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.220
Registry, and to refer
to it going forward

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.240
as, quote-unquote,
"migrate-for-anthos-runtime."

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.100
The next FROM tells
the Docker file

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.720
to use the image
that was generated

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.310
when you ran your migration.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:31.040
It's also stored up in the
Google Container Registry

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.570
under your project.

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.360
Notice that Docker will refer
to this image as, quote unquote,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.250
"source-content."

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:39.560
As an aside, while
I am specifically

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.240
using Google Container
Registry in this example,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.120
depending on where
you're migrating from,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.970
we can also use AWS'
Elastic Container

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.030
Registry or a local registry.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.388
This section is where you
would run any updates,

00:02:52.388 --> 00:02:53.930
and we're going to
cover that in more

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:55.830
detail in the next section.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.160
So for now, we'll
just skip over it.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.010
This line tells Docker to
copy the file system starting

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.800
at the root from the
migrate-for-anthos-runtime

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.400
into the container image.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.860
Now, remember that the container
image we're copying into

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.740
is the source-content image.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.070
So basically, this command
copies the runtime container

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.460
image into the
source-content image.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.230
And the last line
tells the container

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.050
to run the v2k.go
script and start

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.130
your migrated application.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:28.680
Now, as I've said,
this Dockerfile

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.900
is what's used to create
the migrated application

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.860
configuration that you initially
deploy after running Migrate

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.670
for Anthos.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.010
But what do you do
if, down the road,

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.900
you need to update the
image for some reason?

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.880
For example, you need to
install an updated dependency

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.030
or patch your application
or upgrade the underlying

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.520
Migrate for Anthos runtime.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.230
Going back to our
Dockerfile-- now,

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.510
updating the
migrate-for-anthos-runtime is

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.020
actually pretty simple.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:03:58.930
You just go back to
that first FROM line

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.120
and you update the version.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.460
If you need to update any
part of your application,

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.530
including system or
language libraries

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:07.300
or the application
code itself, you

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.680
would do that after the
second FROM statement.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.010
So, for instance, if
I'm a node developer,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.380
I might copy in a
new package.json

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.600
and run "npm install"
to update some

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.390
of the packages my
application is using.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.150
Or I could copy in
a new app.js file

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.400
to update my actual
application code.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.620
The underlying file
system in the container

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:30.080
is the same as what
you had in your VM,

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.850
so you would just
update the files

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.980
in their original locations.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:36.180
Now, from here,
you could manually

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.480
run docker build to
create an updated image,

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.090
push that to GCR,
and then roll out

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:43.440
the new version of
your application.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.720
But manually building
the new container images

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.170
could become tedious if you are
regularly processing updates.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.990
A better approach is to
use a tool like Cloud Build

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.250
to automate the process with
a modern CI/CD pipeline.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.050
By leveraging a tool
like Cloud Build,

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.060
you can define triggers
such that whenever

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.670
a change is pushed to your
source code repository,

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.110
a new container
image is created.

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.030
And once that new
image is created,

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:10.800
you can perform manual
or automated tests.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.400
And then finally, using either
manual or automated processes,

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.670
you can push that code
out into production.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.650
Let's take a look at
how we would implement

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.420
a pipeline in practice.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.300
I'm going to construct
a simple pipeline that

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.520
will connect to my
GitHub repo such

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.230
that when I push a
new change to GitHub,

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.170
a new container image
will be pushed up to GCR.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.450
From there I'll update
my Kubernetes YAML

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:37.160
and roll that updated
app into production.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.480
OK, so let's go ahead here.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.250
We're going to start
in Cloud Build,

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:42.760
and I want to show you the
trigger that I built earlier.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.890
Remember, a trigger is
something that can kick off

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:47.040
via different processes.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.100
So we have a trigger
here that when

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.860
I push to a branch
in my GitHub repo

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.950
here to do a Cloud Build where
I've installed the GitHub

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:56.600
app for Cloud Build.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.000
When I hit the master
branch with an update,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.120
it's going to go ahead
and run a Dockerfile,

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.730
and it's going to do that in
the root of the repository,

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.530
looking for a file
called Dockerfile,

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.110
and it's going to build
us a new image that

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:13.430
is tagged with the short SHA
of the build process, right?

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.120
So that'll be our unique tag.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.030
And then if we look
down here below,

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.820
what we can see is the
actual Dockerfile build-- er,

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.288
the docker build command where
we're going to build that

00:06:22.288 --> 00:06:24.080
and we're going to
point to that Dockerfile

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:24.960
that I just mentioned.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:26.668
So that's what's going
to happen whenever

00:06:26.668 --> 00:06:29.100
I push a change to my repo.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.450
Now, this is the Dockerfile.

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:32.990
We talked about this
earlier in the video.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.450
You can see here is the runtime.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.610
Here is the
non-runnable base that

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:39.620
is created as part of
the migration process.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.560
And then what I have added
to it, if we come down here,

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:46.520
is I'm basically saying, copy
the contents of this repo

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:49.912
into /usr/src/todo in
the container, right,

00:06:49.912 --> 00:06:50.870
in the container image.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.060
And that's where it
was located in the VM.

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.890
That's where it was--
the code was located

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:56.210
in the container, as well then.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.240
They're the same.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.270
So now what I want to
do is I want to come up

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:03.120
and I want to actually go ahead
and make a change to my source

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:03.620
code.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:08.570
So I'm going to come up here
into this showTasks file

00:07:08.570 --> 00:07:10.773
here at the top, and
I'm going to click here.

00:07:10.773 --> 00:07:13.190
And I'm going to go in-- and
I'm not running these on VMs,

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:14.120
so it's not a host.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:15.290
It's actually a pod.

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.990
So let me change this
to pod, and then I

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:20.450
will save this file out.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:23.150
And then I will move down
here into the terminal

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:26.330
and I'll do a git add *
to add the change file.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:28.760
I'll commit that to the
repo with a commit message

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:30.560
of "update host string."

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.960
And then I'll go
ahead and do a push.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.467
Now, this push will
kick off that trigger

00:07:36.467 --> 00:07:37.550
that we just talked about.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:41.110
So if I come into history, you
can see it's already building.

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.870
So we'll come in here
and I'll show you here

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:45.520
in a second when this comes up.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.520
You can actually see
there's that commit message

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.500
I used, "update host string."

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.750
This is going to take a bit,
so let me pause the video

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.950
and I'll rejoin
you when it's done.

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:56.700
OK, so we've finished up here.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.150
And if we come down,
we can see there's

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:02.150
where we copied the source code
into the image, as I mentioned.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:04.280
And here's that
short SHA and we're

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:07.930
going to use that to do the
update to our application.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.070
So let's go to the
image repository here--

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.360
Container Registry.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.620
Look at the images.

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:15.020
And that's where the
non-runnable base gets stored

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.460
by the migration process.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:19.280
And there's the todo directory.

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:22.470
That is where we actually get
our deployable applications.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.600
So there's the one
we just created.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:26.390
And now we're going
to deploy that

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:30.880
by opening up a Cloud Shell
and editing the Kubernetes YAML

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:31.380
file.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.250
So let's open this up
and we're going to move

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:36.380
into the directory where
the artifacts are stored,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.960
and we're going to edit
the deployment_spec.yaml

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.919
and we're just going
to scroll down here,

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.760
and we're going to change
that tag on the image name

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:45.870
to the one we just created.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.210
We're going to save that out.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.180
And then we're going to
go into the terminal,

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.002
and we're going
to go ahead and--

00:08:52.002 --> 00:08:53.960
well, before we do that,
let me point this out.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.430
So it says "served by host."

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:56.847
That's what we're
going to change.

00:08:56.847 --> 00:09:00.140
So now let me go here and I
want to go ahead and clear

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:01.340
the screen.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:05.630
And then we'll do a kubectl
apply -f deployment_spec.yaml,

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:09.740
and that's going to update the
statefulset with the new image.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:12.980
Let's do a get pods with a watch
so we can watch that happen.

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:16.190
And while that's happening,
I want to just talk

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:17.520
about a couple of things.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.160
One, this is not exactly
what you would do, right?

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.160
There would be a step
between building the image

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:25.255
and deploying it, and that would
involve some sort of testing.

00:09:25.255 --> 00:09:26.130
We talked about that.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.120
And the other thing is you
could automate this step

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.165
if you wanted to, right?

00:09:30.165 --> 00:09:31.790
In the video, we
don't really have time

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:35.450
to go through that process, but
you could fully automate this

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:36.680
so you automate your testing.

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:38.870
You automate the
sign-offs and the release,

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.090
and then you automate
the push to production.

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:43.672
Here we're just basically
automating the building

00:09:43.672 --> 00:09:44.630
of the container image.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:47.700
We're assuming a manual test
step and a manual deployment

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:48.200
step.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.175
All right, so we're
almost done here.

00:09:50.175 --> 00:09:51.800
The containers should
be up and running

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.960
in just a couple of seconds.

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:55.870
And looks like it's
just about done.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.370
So let's go and check
on that application

00:09:58.370 --> 00:09:59.930
and see if our
change got reflected.

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.310
So we'll come up here,
click on todo list app,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.730
and you can see down here,
actually there it is.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:07.590
It's now served by a
pod instead of a host.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.090
So the application
has been updated.

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:10.632
So in this video,
we talked about how

00:10:10.632 --> 00:10:13.700
you can use a Cloud Build build
trigger connected to a GitHub

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:17.120
repo to automate the
creation or the updating

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.070
of your migrated applications.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:22.760
That about wraps it
up for this video.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:24.040
I hope you found it useful.

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:26.170
If you want more information
on Migrate for Anthos,

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.570
we have some additional
videos posted here on YouTube,

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:30.790
so check those out
and then head on over

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:34.870
to
cloud.google.com/migrate/anthos

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:36.160
to learn more.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

